  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.25 × loss. It is 4 times steeper in x, so steps overshoot in x when learning rate > 0.5 and grow without bound there, which is the divergence.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63b)) per component for batch size b, as when sampling b of 64 points. It is exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays it.",
    "Arrows are drawn at 2 × the true step length (learning rate × slope estimate). The orange arrow is the step the ball takes next. The grey arrow is learning rate × the true downhill slope. Step length is shown unscaled.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss passes 8 (the loss at the bowl's steep edge) or the ball leaves the drawn bowl (diverged).",
    "Updates come 5 per second while the host is playing. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. The trail holds the start plus one point per step."
  ]
}